{"source":"The reading group has never been especially orderly. People arrive with different editions, someone invariably wants to return to a passage we passed half an hour ago, and the best conversation usually starts just as we ought to finish. I do not think those are reasons to turn it into a class.

We do need a little more structure, though. My suggestion is that we choose the next text before everyone leaves and name a person to open the discussion. That person need not give a lecture or prepare slides. A question, a troublesome paragraph, or an account of why the book annoyed them would be quite enough.","candidate":"The reading group has never been especially orderly. People arrive with different editions, someone invariably wants to go back to a passage we passed half an hour ago, and the best conversation usually starts just as we ought to finish. I don't think any of that is a reason to turn it into a class.

