{"source":"I was sure the old footbridge would be the thing I missed most. It was, for a while. I knew how it sounded when the boards were damp, and I could tell from the light whether the river was running high. Then I noticed I missed knowing which window in the bakery would be open after five, and who would be sitting on the bench even when it was cold. Those details weren't important on their own, but they made the walk feel familiar.

The new place is good. The bus is easier, and I can walk to the market without checking the route first. I just haven't learned its small landmarks yet, which is different from feeling at home. I think I expected that feeling to arrive all at once, like unpacking the last box. Instead, I'm slowly collecting names and routines. Maybe one day I'll know which shop window stays lit late, or recognize the person on the corner bench. For now, I still look for the footbridge in my head when I take the long way home.","candidate":"I was sure the old footbridge would be the thing I missed most. It was, for a while. I knew how it sounded when the boards were damp, and I could tell from the light whether the river was running high. Then I noticed I missed knowing which window in the bakery would be open after five, and who would be sitting on the bench even when it was cold. Those details weren't important on their own, but they made the walk feel familiar.

